{
  "schema_version": "3.0",
  "key": "conditional_clauses",
  "concept_slug": "conditional_clauses",
  "grounding_mode": "fallback_no_wiki",
  "title": "Conditions with hvis",
  "cefr_level": "B1",
  "goal": "Use hvis-clauses to state real and imagined conditions with correct word order.",
  "elements": [
    {
      "kind": "section",
      "role": "orient",
      "title": "What hvis does",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "A conditional sentence has a condition and a result. In Norwegian, "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "hvis",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " introduces the condition. The hvis-clause is a subordinate clause, a clause that cannot stand alone as a complete sentence."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "rule",
          "statement": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Use "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "hvis",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " for a real possible condition: if the condition happens, the result can happen."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Hvis det regner, tar jeg bussen."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "If it rains, I take the bus."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Jeg ringer deg hvis jeg blir forsinket."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "I will call you if I am delayed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "The hvis-clause can come first or second. When it comes second, the first part keeps normal main-clause word order."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "section",
      "role": "model",
      "title": "Word order in and after hvis",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Inside the hvis-clause, Norwegian uses subordinate-clause word order. A sentence adverb, such as "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "ikke",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " or "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "alltid",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": ", normally comes before the finite verb."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "rule",
          "statement": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Inside a hvis-clause: subject plus sentence adverb plus finite verb."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Hvis du ikke kan komme, må du si fra."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "If you cannot come, you must let me know."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Hvis hun alltid jobber sent, blir hun sliten."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "If she always works late, she gets tired."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "When the hvis-clause comes first, the result clause follows V2 word order. V2 means that the finite verb is in second position in a main clause. The whole fronted hvis-clause counts as the first position, so the finite verb comes right after it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "rule",
          "statement": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Fronted condition: "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "Hvis du ikke kan komme, må du si fra.",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " The finite verb "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "må",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " starts the result clause."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Hvis jeg har tid i morgen, besøker jeg deg."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "If I have time tomorrow, I will visit you."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Hvis vi ikke finner adressen, spør vi noen."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "If we do not find the address, we ask someone."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "section",
      "role": "model",
      "title": "Using så after a fronted condition",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Norwegian can add "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "så",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " after a fronted hvis-clause to make the result feel clearly marked. It is common, but it is not required. The verb still comes immediately after "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "så",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " in the result clause."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "rule",
          "statement": [
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "Hvis ... så",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " is a useful frame, but "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "så",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " does not remove the V2 rule."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Hvis du trenger hjelp, så kan jeg komme."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "If you need help, then I can come."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Hvis toget er forsinket, så venter vi på stasjonen."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "If the train is delayed, then we wait at the station."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "callout",
          "level": "note",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "kind": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": "English if can have more than one Norwegian translation. Here, focus on "
                },
                {
                  "kind": "foreign_term",
                  "value": "hvis",
                  "lang": "no"
                },
                {
                  "kind": "text",
                  "value": " when it means a condition."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "section",
      "role": "contrast",
      "title": "Imagined conditions with ville and skulle",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Norwegian often uses modal verbs, verbs such as can, must, would, or should, to show the result of a condition. For imagined or less direct situations, "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "ville",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " works like would, and "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "skulle",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " can work like should or was supposed to."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "rule",
          "statement": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Use "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "ville",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " plus infinitive, the base verb form without tense, for would do something."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Hvis jeg hadde mer tid, ville jeg lære mer norsk."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "If I had more time, I would learn more Norwegian."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Hvis hun bodde nærmere, ville hun besøke oss oftere."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "If she lived closer, she would visit us more often."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "rule",
          "statement": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Use "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "skulle",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " plus infinitive for should do something or was supposed to do something in a conditional frame."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Hvis du skulle bli syk, må du ringe legen."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "If you should become ill, you must call the doctor."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Hvis møtet skulle bli avlyst, sender jeg en melding."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "If the meeting should be cancelled, I will send a message."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "section",
      "role": "recap",
      "title": "Quick recap",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "For conditions, build the sentence around "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "hvis",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": ". Then check two places: the word order inside the hvis-clause, and the verb position in the result clause."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Inside the hvis-clause, put sentence adverbs before the finite verb: "
              },
              {
                "kind": "foreign_term",
                "value": "hvis du ikke kan",
                "lang": "no"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "When the hvis-clause comes first, put the finite verb early in the result: "
              },
              {
                "kind": "foreign_term",
                "value": "Hvis jeg har tid, kommer jeg.",
                "lang": "no"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Use "
              },
              {
                "kind": "foreign_term",
                "value": "ville",
                "lang": "no"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " for would in imagined results."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Use "
              },
              {
                "kind": "foreign_term",
                "value": "skulle",
                "lang": "no"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " for should or was supposed to in conditional frames."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "kind": "foreign_term",
                "value": "så",
                "lang": "no"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " can mark the result after a fronted condition, but the verb still follows the V2 pattern."
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
